### Reject unusable inspector ports

**Severity:** `medium`

The manifest currently accepts zero, negative values, and values above the TCP port range. Those values reach process launch and produce an extension that cannot expose the requested inspector endpoint. Validate an explicitly supplied port while parsing, and add regression cases for `-1`, `0`, and `65536`.

```suggestion
        // An explicit inspector port must be usable as a TCP port. Port 0 is not
        // supported because the host must know which port the extension selected.
        if (package.CmdPal.DebugPort is <= 0 or > 65535)
        {
            return JSExtensionManifestParseResult.Failure("The 'cmdpal.debugPort' field must be between 1 and 65535.");
        }

        // Rule 3: either cmdpal.main or top-level main must resolve to an existing file.
```

### Fail closed when an entry-point segment disappears

**Severity:** `high`

A file or directory can disappear after the earlier existence check but before its attributes are read. Returning `false` here treats that unverified segment as safe, so parsing can accept an entry point whose containment was never established. Reject the path when the filesystem race prevents verification.

```suggestion
            // The entry point was present before validation began. If a segment disappears during
            // the walk, fail closed rather than trusting a path whose attributes were not verified.
            return true;
```

### Serialize side-effecting RPC registration

**Severity:** `high`

`ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd` may run its value factory more than once for the same key. Here the factory registers a local RPC method as a side effect, so concurrent handler registration can create duplicate or ambiguous targets even if only one dictionary value wins. Serialize the check, registration, and update as one operation.

```suggestion
        lock (_registeredMethods)
        {
            if (_registeredMethods.ContainsKey(method))
            {
                return;
            }

            _registeredMethods[method] = AddRpcMethodTarget(method, this);
        }
```

### Retry notification admission after a consumer race

**Severity:** `high`

When the first `TryWrite` observes a full queue, the consumer can remove the last item before this producer calls `TryRead`. The current `return` then silently drops the new notification without incrementing `DroppedNotificationCount`. Retry while the channel is open, yielding so the consumer can make progress.

```suggestion
            if (_notificationQueue.Reader.Completion.IsCompleted)
            {
                return;
            }

            Thread.Yield();
```

### Isolate disconnect event subscribers

**Severity:** `medium`

A throwing `Disconnected` subscriber currently prevents every later subscriber from observing transport teardown. Invoke handlers independently, matching the error-event isolation already used in this class, and add coverage with a throwing first subscriber followed by an observing subscriber.

```suggestion
            var handlers = Disconnected;
            if (handlers is null)
            {
                return;
            }

            foreach (EventHandler handler in handlers.GetInvocationList())
            {
                try
                {
                    handler(this, EventArgs.Empty);
                }
                catch (Exception ex)
                {
                    Logger.LogError("A JSON-RPC disconnected event handler failed.", ex);
                }
            }
```
